(* The /audit workflow, as a grammar.

   Source for docs/images/audit-workflow.svg, embedded in
   docs/agent-system.md. Rendered with
   https://ys-math.github.io/syntax-diagram-generator/ — paste this file in,
   choose EBNF (ISO/IEC 14977), set Fit to Wrap, and export the SVG.

   One rule, as in the other two grammars: the generator draws one diagram per
   rule, and the point of this picture is that a run is a single path with two
   places it can stop.

   The two optionals nest rather than sit side by side, and that nesting is the
   whole subject. Naming findings buys a plan; only "Y" buys an edit. A run that
   stops at either gate has still written the report, which is why the report
   sits outside both.

   "Y" is the one quoted terminal in this repo that is not a slash command. It
   is quoted because it is literal: the gate is satisfied by that keystroke and
   by nothing that has to be interpreted. scripts/test_agent_docs.py matches
   quoted terminals beginning with a slash, so it checks "/audit" and "/git"
   here and passes over "Y".

   Special sequences (? … ?) are the steps that are not a command: reading,
   judging, fixing, rolling back. The rollback is drawn as the second branch
   rather than an exception, because a failed check ends one fix and not the
   run. *)

audit =
    "/audit" ,
    ? run the script tests ? ,
    ? read every layer ? ,
    ? write the report ? ,
    ? print the index ? ,
    [ ? name the findings to fix ? ,
      ? print the execution plan ? ,
      [ "Y" ,
        { ? apply one fix ? ,
          ( ? tests and hooks green ? | ? git restore that fix ? ) } ,
        ? tick what was applied ? ,
        ? reprint the index ? ] ] ,
    [ "/git" ] ;
